WEBVTT

00:00.330 --> 00:04.539
On Tuesday, your colleague Rowan says, “Your idea looks promising.”

00:05.580 --> 00:10.236
On Thursday, during a meeting, Rowan says, “I don’t think it’s ready yet.”

00:11.247 --> 00:12.828
You might feel embarrassed.

00:13.237 --> 00:17.128
You might think, “Rowan wanted to make me look bad in front of everyone.”

00:18.163 --> 00:22.462
But notice something: an idea can be promising and still need work.

00:22.859 --> 00:26.405
Rowan’s two statements are not necessarily contradictory.

00:27.455 --> 00:28.845
You know what Rowan said.

00:29.229 --> 00:31.499
You do not yet know why Rowan said it.

00:31.908 --> 00:33.209
The guess could be right.

00:33.604 --> 00:36.665
For now, keep it separate from the words you heard.

00:37.705 --> 00:39.261
Your feelings matter too.

00:39.658 --> 00:43.408
Saying “I felt embarrassed” describes how the meeting affected you.

00:43.790 --> 00:47.043
It does not establish whether Rowan meant to embarrass you.

00:48.080 --> 00:52.391
Before deciding what to do, consider another possible explanation.

00:52.762 --> 00:56.920
Perhaps Rowan discovered a budget problem after Tuesday’s conversation.

00:57.303 --> 01:00.798
Perhaps Rowan liked the idea but thought it needed more work.

01:01.206 --> 01:03.527
You understood “promising” to mean “ready”.

01:03.936 --> 01:07.635
Or perhaps Rowan wanted to protect their reputation in the meeting.

01:08.663 --> 01:10.653
These are possibilities to check.

01:11.062 --> 01:12.797
We do not know which is right.

01:13.192 --> 01:14.787
More than one could be true.

01:15.182 --> 01:19.621
A useful question to remember is: “What do I know, and what am I guessing?”

01:20.663 --> 01:24.413
Two kinds of bias can make this distinction harder to maintain.

01:24.823 --> 01:27.094
The first is confirmation bias.

01:27.502 --> 01:32.081
This means looking for information—or understanding it in a way—that supports

01:32.094 --> 01:33.344
what you already believe.

01:34.372 --> 01:37.076
Suppose you think Rowan dislikes your idea.

01:37.485 --> 01:41.031
You notice each criticism but overlook positive comments.

01:41.439 --> 01:44.602
That makes your existing belief seem better supported.

01:45.010 --> 01:47.090
You may not realise you are doing it.

01:48.122 --> 01:52.624
Psychologist Raymond Nickerson examined earlier research on this pattern in a

01:52.637 --> 01:54.907
paper published in 1998.

01:55.317 --> 01:59.283
That research helps explain the idea; it cannot tell us whether your

01:59.296 --> 02:01.248
conclusion about Rowan is right.

02:02.288 --> 02:04.686
The second is correspondence bias.

02:05.095 --> 02:09.292
This means judging someone’s character from their behaviour while giving too little

02:09.305 --> 02:10.963
weight to their circumstances.

02:11.997 --> 02:17.265
For example, you decide Rowan is an unhelpful person after one critical comment.

02:17.674 --> 02:21.590
You have not checked whether a new budget problem affected the proposal.

02:21.972 --> 02:25.722
Gilbert and Malone examined earlier research on this bias in a

02:25.735 --> 02:28.018
paper published in 1995.

02:29.038 --> 02:30.786
The two ideas are different.

02:31.156 --> 02:34.855
Confirmation bias concerns how you handle information.

02:35.225 --> 02:40.391
Correspondence bias concerns how you explain behaviour: you may judge the person while

02:40.403 --> 02:41.896
overlooking their situation.

02:42.913 --> 02:44.597
So what could you ask Rowan?

02:45.006 --> 02:46.677
“Why did you try to embarrass me?”

02:47.111 --> 02:49.891
assumes the intention you are trying to find out about.

02:50.274 --> 02:54.636
Instead, you could ask: “On Tuesday you said the idea looked promising.

02:55.019 --> 02:56.856
Today you said it wasn’t ready.

02:57.251 --> 02:58.603
What needs more work?”

02:58.986 --> 02:59.904
Then listen.

03:00.955 --> 03:05.126
If you are still unsure, ask yourself: “What would help me check that?”

03:05.535 --> 03:10.152
If Rowan mentions staffing costs, you could ask, “Can we look at the figures?”

03:10.561 --> 03:15.433
If Rowan says the proposal is unfinished, you could ask, “What still needs doing?”

03:16.455 --> 03:19.185
These questions ask for something specific.

03:19.569 --> 03:22.732
They do not guarantee an honest answer or agreement.

03:23.101 --> 03:27.412
Compare the answer with relevant information before deciding what to do.

03:27.821 --> 03:31.048
You can still feel hurt while asking a useful question.

03:32.080 --> 03:34.414
Now consider a different situation.

03:34.823 --> 03:37.859
A friend hasn’t replied to your message for two days.

03:38.267 --> 03:40.474
You think, “They don’t care about me.”

03:41.497 --> 03:45.183
Pause the video and answer three questions: What do you know?

03:45.593 --> 03:46.690
What are you guessing?

03:47.059 --> 03:48.742
What could you ask or check?

03:53.830 --> 03:56.674
You know your friend has not replied for two days.

03:57.084 --> 04:00.094
You are guessing that this means they do not care about you.

04:01.122 --> 04:02.576
They might be avoiding you.

04:02.946 --> 04:06.645
They might also be busy, unwell or unsure what to say.

04:07.054 --> 04:09.490
You could ask, “Did you see my message?”

04:09.898 --> 04:12.118
That checks something you do not yet know.

04:12.500 --> 04:15.319
It does not settle everything about the friendship.

04:16.330 --> 04:19.200
There is an important limit to all this questioning.

04:19.609 --> 04:23.066
You do not have to discover someone’s motive before responding to

04:23.078 --> 04:24.737
repeated harmful behaviour.

04:25.747 --> 04:29.050
Where it is safe, you can request a specific change.

04:29.459 --> 04:32.699
You could ask Rowan to raise problems before a meeting so you

04:32.712 --> 04:34.077
have time to address them.

04:35.122 --> 04:40.071
If asking directly could put you at risk, seek appropriate support rather than using a

04:40.083 --> 04:41.946
conversation to test the person.

04:42.329 --> 04:45.595
Understanding someone does not require accepting harm.

04:46.622 --> 04:51.213
To practise, choose one ordinary situation from your own life this week.

04:51.611 --> 04:54.876
Write three short answers: “What did they say or do?”

04:55.284 --> 04:56.853
“Why do I think they did it?”

04:57.248 --> 04:58.358
“How could I check?”

04:58.766 --> 05:01.470
If it is safe and useful, make that check.

05:02.497 --> 05:04.691
Afterwards, note what you learned.

05:05.061 --> 05:08.505
Did it change your explanation, or what you will do next?

05:08.914 --> 05:11.962
You may still be uncertain about the person’s motive.

05:12.370 --> 05:16.567
You may nevertheless have enough information to choose a useful next step.
